   519	**Round 137's corrections (D599).**
   520	
   521	- **Every reader opens a store by its own path.** `store.open_read_only`, which the API, MCP, `service` and reports all use,
   522	  resolves a symlink and refuses a store with another hard link (`live_lock.LockRefused`, naming the reason).
   523	  - The API refuses at start (`index_at_start`) and per request (500, the reason in the body).
   524	  - MCP refuses at start and per tool call.
   525	  - Census before the change: none of the 702 `.sqlite` files under `poc/data` and `clean-arch` has a second hard link.
   526	- **Cleanup when cleanup itself fails:**
   527	  - **`LadderAdapter.close`:** if leaving WAL mode fails, the connection is still closed and then the lock released. If closing
   528	    the connection fails, the lock is kept until the process ends (the operating system releases it then). So a writable
   529	    connection never outlives its lock.
   530	  - **`snapshot`:** if the target cannot be opened, the source is closed and nothing is left.
   531	  - **`scripts/snapshot_store.py`** also reports a SQLite error as a refusal (exit 1) instead of a traceback.
   532	- **A store moved mid-run, a finding, not understood and not fixed:**
   533	  - What was seen: SQLite raised "disk I/O error" on the writer's next statement after its store file was renamed, before the
   534	    writer's own path check. This happened only in a process that had first run the API/MCP hard-link test. Which SQLite state in
   535	    that process makes the difference was not identified.
   536	  - A moved store breaks the contract's operating precondition (a store's path is never replaced while it may be in use). This
   537	    finding is therefore a stated limit of that test case, not a claim that the writer handles a moved store.
   538	  - What the writer records no longer depends on it:
   539	    - its stop takes the last completed day from its loop;
   540	    - before each day it checks the path first, then any checkpoint failure.
   541	
   542	**Round 138's correction (D600): a reader holding the store is reported apart from every other failure.**
   543	
   544	- `truncate_checkpoint` answers `done`, `busy` or the error's own text. `busy` means SQLite's own "database is locked" messages,
   545	  or a checkpoint held back by another connection. That shows contention, not which connection; round 139 renamed the recorded
   546	  reasons to "another connection held the store".
   547	  - Only `busy` counts toward the three-day stop "another connection held the store".
   548	  - Any other error stops the writer before the next day with "the truncating checkpoint after <day> failed: <error>". After the
   549	    last day it is recorded in the counts.
   550	- `leave_wal` answers the mode it ends in and why WAL was kept: "another connection held the store at the end", or "leaving WAL
   551	  failed: <error>".
   552	- Tested with a connection whose checkpoint, or whose change out of WAL, fails with "disk I/O error":
   553	  - the stop's reason, and the record at the end, name that error and not a reader;
   554	  - the lock is free afterwards.
   555	- The real held-reader test still reports a reader.
